{"name":"Indian Peafowl","hex":"#0044aa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"indian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigene”)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigenous”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/indian"},{"term":"peafowl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bird of the genus Pavo or Afropavo, notable for the extravagant tails of the males; a peacock (unspecified sex)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peafowl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Indian peafowl","blocks":[{"text":"The Indian peafowl, also known as the common peafowl, or blue peafowl, is a species of peafowl native to the Indian subcontinent. While it originated in the Indian subcontinent, it has since been introduced to many other parts of the world. Male peafowl are referred to as peacocks, and female peafowl are referred to as peahens, although both sexes are often referred to colloquially as a \"peacock\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Peacock_on_tree_%2852077240794%29.jpg/330px-Peacock_on_tree_%2852077240794%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_peafowl","wikidata":"Q61865"},{"title":"Peafowl","blocks":[{"text":"Peafowl is a common name for two bird species of the genus Pavo and one species of the closely related genus Afropavo within the tribe Pavonini of the family Phasianidae. Male peafowl are referred to as peacocks, and female peafowl are referred to as peahens. Despite this, peacock is usually used to refer to both sexes, in common language."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Peacock_Plumage.jpg/330px-Peacock_Plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peafowl","wikidata":"Q201251"}],"images":[{"sr